---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Enforcement & takedowns"
  description="How a Kirpik Proof of Ownership Certificate supports a DMCA notice, a hosting complaint and removal from Google when your Shopify text is stolen. Not legal advice."
>
  <PageHero
    title="The Enforcement Guide"
    lead="You found your text on another site and Kirpik confirmed the watermark. This guide covers what happens next: the certificate, the DMCA notice, the hosting complaint and removal from Google."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container enf">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Successful takedowns require proof</h2>
        <p>
          The companies that host websites and the search engines that list them process complaints in volume.
          The person reading yours has never heard of your store and cannot
          know who wrote what. What moves them is a specific, checkable
          demonstration that the text is yours and predates the theft.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is a harder bar for text than for images. Two product
          descriptions can legitimately resemble each other, and "we sell the
          same thing" is a common defence. The evidence must rule that defence
          out: not "these pages are similar" but "this exact text carries
          this store's watermark, was protected on this date, and appeared on
          the infringing page later".
        </p>

        <h2>What a Proof of Ownership Certificate contains</h2>
        <p>
          When Kirpik detects your watermark in a suspect page, you can generate
          a Proof of Ownership Certificate from that detection: a dated
          ownership document built for the people who handle these complaints.
          Each certificate records:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A unique certificate ID, the scope of the document, your date of protection and the Protection Signature recorded for your original content</li>
          <li>Your original page and the infringing page, with the detection date and the dates the infringing page itself reports</li>
          <li>The earliest Internet Archive snapshot of the infringing page, a record neither side controls</li>
          <li>The recovered Watermark Security Markers, the Confidence figure and a side-by-side comparison of your original and the modified version</li>
          <li>A QR code linking to a public verification page, so anyone can confirm the certificate is genuine without trusting the PDF</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The point of all that detail is simple: the person handling your
          complaint should be able to verify your claim in minutes, from their
          desk, without taking your word for anything.
        </p>

        <h2>Route one: the DMCA notice</h2>
        <p>
          For most stolen text the first move is a DMCA takedown notice sent to
          the company that hosts the infringing site. A valid notice identifies
          your original work, identifies the infringing material by URL, and
          includes the required statements and your signature. Attach the
          certificate PDF or include its public verification link as your
          supporting evidence. Text is fully protected by copyright the moment
          you write it, so product descriptions, blog articles and pages all
          qualify.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once sent, the hosting company reviews the notice and either removes
          the material or forwards the notice to the site owner, who can
          formally dispute it. Our guide
          <a href="/learn/dmca-for-stolen-text/">The DMCA process for stolen
          text, step by step</a> walks through finding the right recipient,
          wording each required element, and what to do if the site owner
          disputes your notice (a counter-notice).
        </p>

        <h2>Route two: the hosting complaint</h2>
        <p>
          Some sites sit with hosting companies that operate their own
          complaint procedures alongside or instead of the DMCA, especially
          outside the United States. The approach is the same: find the
          company that hosts the site (the DMCA guide above shows how), find
          its complaints address, and send the infringing web address together
          with your certificate. Hosting companies vary widely in speed, and a
          verifiable evidence package gives their team something they can act
          on.
        </p>

        <h2>Route three: removal from Google</h2>
        <p>
          Sometimes the practical damage is not that the stolen page exists but
          that it ranks. Google accepts copyright removal requests for search
          results, and a successful request removes the infringing page from
          results even while the page itself stays up. For a store whose stolen
          content is outranking the original, this can be the quickest relief,
          and you can file it alongside a DMCA notice rather than instead of
          one. See <a href="/learn/deindexing-stolen-copy/">Getting stolen
          content removed from Google's index</a> for the process.
        </p>

        <h2>When to bring in a specialist</h2>
        <p>
          A well-evidenced notice is often the last step needed. When it is
          not, the infringer hides behind privacy services, reposts under a
          new domain, or ignores everything. For those cases, we recommend
          engaging an intellectual property lawyer or a takedown specialist
          rather than handling it alone. Arrive with your Proof of Ownership
          Certificate and the notices you have already sent, and the
          fact-finding is already done.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="enf__side">
        <div class="card sidecard sidecard--steps">
          <h3>The playbook</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Detect your watermark in the suspect page</li>
            <li>Generate the certificate from the detection</li>
            <li>Send the DMCA notice or hosting complaint</li>
            <li>Request removal from Google if the stolen page ranks</li>
            <li>Bring in a specialist if the infringer ignores you</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="card sidecard">
          <h3>The certificate only exists if you protected first</h3>
          <p>Kirpik can only certify text that carried your watermark before it was copied. Protection is the step you cannot do retroactively.</p>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost sidecard__btn" href="/how-it-works/">How protection works</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card sidecard sidecard--muted">
          <p>This page is general information about how takedown processes work, written by a software company, not a law firm. It is not legal advice, and enforcement rules differ between countries. For anything contested or high-stakes, consult a qualified professional in your jurisdiction.</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand sub="Enable real protection and actual enforcement for your products, articles, blogs and pages. Install Kirpik today.">
    <Fragment slot="heading">Start protecting your content <em>now</em>.</Fragment>
  </CtaBand>
</Base>

<style>
  .enf { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.7fr 1fr; gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem); align-items: start; }
  .enf__side { display: grid; gap: 1.1rem; position: sticky; top: 96px; }
  .sidecard { padding: 1.5rem; }
  .sidecard h3 { margin: 0.6rem 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .sidecard p { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .sidecard__btn { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.92rem; padding: 0.75em 1.1em; }
  .sidecard--steps ol { margin: 0.4rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: 0.55rem; }
  .sidecard--steps li { color: var(--ink-soft); padding-left: 0.2rem; }
  .sidecard--steps li::marker { color: var(--orange); font-weight: 600; font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .sidecard--muted { background: var(--paper-2); box-shadow: none; }
  @media (max-width: 880px) { .enf { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .enf__side { position: static; } }
</style>
